$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM General/Typical Procedure: General procedure of Hofmann rearrangement A
typical procedure is as follows; To a solution of benzamide (100 mg, 0.82
mmol) and AgOAc (160 mg, 0.98 mmol) in 5 ml of DMF, methanol (1 ml) was
added, followed by addition of a solution of NBP (216 mg, 0.98 mmol) in 3
ml of DMF at 0°C under Ar atmosphere. After the reaction mixture was
stirred for 2 hrs at 0°C and 10 hrs at room temperature. This reaction
mixture was evaporated under reduced pressure; the residue was diluted
with 300 ml of EtOAc; washed with H2O (30 cc X 2) 5% HCl (30 cc X 2);
dried over anhydrous MgSO4; and evaporated to give 125 mg of yellow
semisolid. This was purified with silica gel column chromatography
(hexane: EtOAc=3 : 1) to give 121 mg of amorphorous solid. Methyl
N-benzylcarbamate Phenylacetamide (100 mg), AgOAc (146 mg), NBP (197 mg),
methanol (1 ml); yield; quant (100%), solid. mp. 62-63°C IR (nujol) cm-1
3320 (NH), 1700 (CO); NMR (CDCl3) δ : 7.30 (s, 5H, C6H5), 5.00 (br, 1H,
NH), 4.36 (d, 2H, CH2, J = 6 Hz), 3.70 (s, 3H, CH3O)
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM HOFMANN REARRANGEMENT
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Hofmann rearrangement by using N-bromophthalimide-silver acetate in DMF
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Park, Min Soo; Choi, Chang Uk
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Archives of Pharmacal Research (1993), 16(2), 152-4

